** Item Lore: Magic Item Use Skill

Affects the use of wands and scrolls, some magical
tools, and alchemy. At unskilled level things go awry
more often. Basic is current rules. Skilled you get
some small perks, bigger ones at Expert. You gain
practice points with every use of an expendable
item. For maximums, something like this:

Arc Bar Cav Hea Kni Mon Pri Ran Rog Sam Tou Val Wiz
 S   U   U   S   B   B   B   S   E   U   B   B   E

Starting level would be determined by the presence of
items in the inventory (as with weapons), so one class's
basic might be better than another's. Balance issues
are fun to consider depending on how bad Unskilled is
and how fast one can progress.

In a simple implementation, at Unskilled you
would have a small chance of getting the effect
bumped down a BUC level (so unskilled uncursed wand
use can result in explosion). Or, less viciously,
you sometimes get the confused effect by accident.
At skilled, you would get a small chance of an uncursed
being promoted to a blessed. (There are times when you
want the cursed effect, but are there times when you
prefer the uncursed over blessed?). At expert you would
have a higher chance, and perhaps current wizard perks
stolen from wizards then given back through this
mechanism.

Less simply, the effects could vary with the item
or item class being used. An idea I find amusing for
expert level:

What do you want to read? s
Improvise? y
<You read the scroll as if confused>

Another experty power might be something like the ability
to use color alchemy. 

A different way to come at this YANI without adding a new
skill is to use the current spellcasting skills, and pair
up magic items with schools (?oT->Escape, /oW->Matter,
and so on).

** Skill Overtraining

This YANI makes it possible to exceed the normal skill
maximums, but at much increased cost of training and
(especially) skill slots. For this YANI to work, the
costs have to be nearly prohibitive or the result will
be a blurring blandness of the roles.

The following table or something like it would apply
when trying to train to a level past the normal maximum.

Level    Hits   Slots
~~~~~    ~~~~   ~~~~~
Basic    320      6
Skilled  500      9
Expert   720     14

It's just barely possible to reach expert in a restricted
weapon (for some reason that possibility seems necessary).
I would also apply the table unchanged to the other
skills, leaving Master and Grand Master still unattainable.

** Fighting style balance

This YANI is: when using a 2H weapon, strength damage bonus
is doubled. When using a single 1H weapon and no shield,
the character has a chance of getting an extra attack with
the weapon (depending on skill level). Someone with more
love of damage calculations would have to come up with the
numbers to make things interesting between twoweapon, shield,
two-handed, single weapon, and martial arts styles. 

** New Artifacts

New artifacts could be simply added, but the starting idea
for this YANI was to remove the ability to wish for quest
rewards and make some of those powers available in artifacts
that are more widely useable but not as thumpingly spiffy
as some of the quest artifacts are. Some other ideas crept in.
Also what might go along with this YANI is the idea of /oW =
3 wishes exactly, no more no less.

